# Silas's Thread of Fate at camp (Liberation): the beats that wait for a rest (story/camp_talk.gd offers the first `talk_` node whose opening `if` holds,
# once a playthrough). Order matters: the Incitement's echo, the Discovery, the Confrontation (held back while Silas is Strained until the
# `talk_confrontation_wait` word has been had, never for good). Rewards come from Fateweaving when each beat's quest ends (story/fateweaving.gd).
# Drafted by Gemini (docs/plans/gemini_companions/), edited; every edit is listed in the README there. Lines are unvoiced until the voice lane reads them.

~ talk_incitement
if name:silas_vane and flag.pending_liberation_incitement and not flag.incitement_liberation
-> incitement
endif
-> END

~ incitement
Narrator: Silas crouches near the fire, feedin' dry wormwood stems into his brass censer until a ribbon of green smoke curls round his ribs.
interject name:silas_vane: Doubled back to that wrecked mail car while you lot were catching your breath, friend, and found an old bill waiting in the spilled sacks.
interject name:silas_vane: Brass-cornered envelope with a skull-and-spade seal, blank as bone until my own lagging shadow leaned over the page and drew the ink right out.
interject name:silas_vane: Some dead clerk left a map to a house table where a bad stake can be won back if your own shadow helps you cheat the shoe.
interject name:silas_vane: I have been dodging that tab for years, and holding the paper made my ribs feel ten winters colder.
* Who sent the summons? -> incitement_1
* Keep your eyes on the road ahead. -> incitement_2

~ incitement_1
Narrator: Silas taps the edge of his bone tarot deck against his knee, his green eye-lights narrowing through the bitter smoke.
interject name:silas_vane: We call him the Dealer, and he never posts a card unless he expects you to sit down and lose the rest of your skin.
interject name:silas_vane: Still, if there is a crooked table waiting down the line, I would rather bring my own nerve than let his lookout drag me there.
set incitement_liberation
quest connection_liberation waiting
set silas_inc_choice = "shown"
inspire name:silas_vane: read a dead clerk's warning
-> END

~ incitement_2
interject name:silas_vane: Fair play, no sense crying over a marked hand before the cards even hit the baize.
interject name:silas_vane: Just do not mind the extra smoke tonight while I keep my little shadow blind.
set incitement_liberation
quest connection_liberation waiting
set silas_inc_choice = "pocketed"
inspire name:silas_vane: read a dead clerk's warning
-> END

~ talk_discovery
if name:silas_vane and flag.connection_liberation and flag.crossroads_done and not flag.discovery_liberation
-> discovery
endif
-> END

~ discovery
Narrator: Silas sits on an upturned crate by the embers, turning a single bone card over his knuckles while his shadow stretches flat and still behind him.
interject name:silas_vane: Walked right into the Drowned Crossroads and found the exact green baize where my old tab was written, friend, still smelling of canal rot and cheap wax.
interject name:silas_vane: Same silver shoe humming on the cloth, same cold ledger waiting to collect, like the house never closed the game after I ran.
interject name:silas_vane: I used to think the Dealer wrote an airtight contract, no exit in the margins, just pay the blood stake or fold.
Narrator: He stops the card between two fingerbones and lets out a quick, dry breath through his teeth.
interject name:silas_vane: Then my own spying shadow slipped a card off the bottom of the deck and pointed out the fine print tucked behind the seal.
interject name:silas_vane: Turns out any bet taken at that table can be called back for a straight draw if the house lookout turns partner and backs the player's hand.
interject name:silas_vane: My shadow is dealing for me now, which means the loophole is real and the ledger can actually be opened.
interject name:silas_vane: Only catch is sitting back down at that cloth means looking straight at the marker I left behind when I walked away the first time.
* What else was written on that marker? -> discovery_1
* Then we use the loophole and clean out the house. -> discovery_2
* You found what you needed. Get some rest. -> discovery_3

~ discovery_1
Narrator: The green pinpoints in Silas's sockets dip toward the ash, and his thumb rubs the front of his breastbone through his waistcoat.
interject name:silas_vane: Just a date and a price that never should have been mine to spend, and that is all the air I am giving it tonight.
interject name:silas_vane: When the time comes to pull that page out of the book, I will make sure the tally comes out clean.
approve silas_vane +2: You asked what else was written on my marker.
set discovery_liberation
quest discovery_liberation done
quest confrontation_liberation waiting
-> END

~ discovery_2
Narrator: Silas lets out a short, raspy laugh, snapping the card back into his pack with a one-handed flourish.
interject name:silas_vane: Now that is proper table talk, no trembling over the buy-in when the shoe is finally tipping our way.
interject name:silas_vane: Let the house count its chips for now, because next time we sit down we are taking the whole pot back.
approve silas_vane +3: You backed my play to clean out the house.
set discovery_liberation
quest discovery_liberation done
quest confrontation_liberation waiting
-> END

~ discovery_3
interject name:silas_vane: Right you are, a hustler who stays up admiring his own hand usually gets his pockets picked by dawn.
interject name:silas_vane: I will tuck the clause away and keep my knuckles ready for morning.
approve silas_vane -1: You told me to rest after hearing my news.
set discovery_liberation
quest discovery_liberation done
quest confrontation_liberation waiting
-> END

~ talk_confrontation_wait
if name:silas_vane and flag.discovery_liberation and flag.chuckles_dead and not flag.confrontation_liberation and approval.silas_vane <= strained and not flag.silas_vane_confrontation_waited
-> confrontation_wait
endif
-> END

~ confrontation_wait
Narrator: Silas stands a few paces back from the firelight, his brass censer hangin' cold on its chain while his jaw clicks once against the damp.
interject name:silas_vane: Something big went down back at Fool's Day, friend, right across the sawdust where the house thought it had me boxed.
interject name:silas_vane: I would lay the whole hand out for you tonight, only I like knowing the people at my elbow will not sell a poor sod's skin to the bailiff.
interject name:silas_vane: Lately your play has been a bit too cozy with bad bargains, and I do not show my hole card at a table I cannot read.
interject name:silas_vane: Let us see how we walk the next mile or two before I open my waistcoat on this one.
* Fair enough. We will do better by you. -> confrontation_wait_1
* Keep your secrets if you want. -> confrontation_wait_2
* Say nothing. Stay by the fire. -> confrontation_wait_3

~ confrontation_wait_1
Narrator: Silas loosens his shoulders a fraction and flips a pinch of dry herb into the brass grate.
interject name:silas_vane: Tell's in the twitch, and you sounded straight enough just then.
interject name:silas_vane: Give it a little road dust to settle, and I will tell you how we called the bluff.
approve silas_vane +6: You owned your missteps and promised to do better.
set silas_vane_confrontation_waited
-> END

~ confrontation_wait_2
interject name:silas_vane: Suit yourself, a quiet table saves breath for both of us.
interject name:silas_vane: Card will still be in my pocket if the wind changes and you feel like talking square.
approve silas_vane -2: You told me to keep my secrets to myself.
set silas_vane_confrontation_waited
-> END

~ confrontation_wait_3
Narrator: Silas watches the sparks rise into the dark for a beat, then nods once and steps back toward his bedroll.
interject name:silas_vane: Night is long enough without forcing the deal.
set silas_vane_confrontation_waited
-> END

~ talk_confrontation
if name:silas_vane and flag.discovery_liberation and flag.chuckles_dead and not flag.confrontation_liberation and (approval.silas_vane > strained or flag.silas_vane_confrontation_waited)
-> confrontation
endif
-> END

~ confrontation
Narrator: Silas sits close to the flames with his brass censer unhooked beside his boots, while the small shadow at his heel lies quiet and still.
interject name:silas_vane: Back at Fool's Day, behind all the sour smoke and painted streamers, the Dealer finally pushed his own voice across the counter to name his price.
interject name:silas_vane: He thought flashing the old ledger in the dark would make me scramble for a dirty trick, same as I always have.
interject name:silas_vane: Every hustle I ever pulled was just noise to keep from hearing that first game.
Narrator: He rests his bony palms flat on his knees, no cards in his fingers and no patter in his throat.
interject name:silas_vane: House always wins, unless you burn the house, or unless you stop shaking long enough to look the swindler in the eye.
interject name:silas_vane: So I called his bluff flat out across the sawdust and told him to bring the whole ledger to the table when the last hand gets dealt.
interject name:silas_vane: Second I laid the challenge down, the chain on my censer went cold and the little shadow at my heel stopped twitching for its old master.
interject name:silas_vane: No more lookout spying on my sleeve, just me and a straight draw coming for everything I owe.
interject name:silas_vane: Tell me straight, when a gambler throws away his stacked deck, do you reckon he still has a prayer of winning an honest hand?
* What happens when that last hand comes due? -> confrontation_1
* You will beat him straight, and we will be there. -> confrontation_2
* Save your strength for the road tomorrow. -> confrontation_3

~ confrontation_1
Narrator: Silas picks up a glowing coal with two bare fingerbones and drops it into the censer just to watch the warm light.
interject name:silas_vane: Then the pages go down on the cloth, and there is nowhere left to palm a card or hide behind the smoke.
interject name:silas_vane: Either I read his tell with clean hands and walk everybody out of that book, or the tab closes on me for good.
approve silas_vane +4: You asked what comes next when the last hand is dealt.
set confrontation_liberation
quest confrontation_liberation done
-> END

~ confrontation_2
Narrator: A quick, crooked grin returns to Silas's jaw, and his small shadow raises one hand in a silent salute against the logs.
interject name:silas_vane: Never thought I would hear myself backing an honest draw, but with this crew at the table I might actually like the odds.
interject name:silas_vane: Let the old cheat bring his ledger whenever he is ready, because we are not folding an inch.
approve silas_vane +6: You trusted me to win the final hand straight.
set confrontation_liberation
quest confrontation_liberation done
-> END

~ confrontation_3
interject name:silas_vane: Always practical, and here I was getting sentimental over a quiet shadow.
interject name:silas_vane: The bluff is called and the stake is set, so I will take my rest while the brass is cool.
approve silas_vane -2: You kept things brisk and told me to rest.
set confrontation_liberation
quest confrontation_liberation done
-> END
